  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Colder air will be accompanied by mainly scattered \nwet snow and rain showers through the weekend with minor snow \naccumulations expected across the higher terrain.\n\nA general cold north to northwesterly flow will continue to produce \nsome upslope and lake-effect precip through tonight south of the \nLower Great Lakes. This being said...the short fetch across the \nlakes...shallow nature of the background synoptic moisture...and \nrelatively low inversion heights should help to keep this strictly \nof the light/nuisance variety. The marginally cold airmass will \nresult in primarily wet snow across the higher terrain...with a \ndaytime rain/wet snow mix across the lower elevations reverting back \nto mainly snow tonight with nocturnal cooling of the boundary layer. \nMost accumulations through tonight will on the order of an inch or \nless and mainly confined to the higher terrain...with little or no \nsnow accumulation across the bulk of the lower elevations. \n\nOn Saturday generally light/nuisance lake effect and upslope pcpn \nshould continue into the morning...before temporarily undergoing a \nbrief uptick during the afternoon and early evening hours as a \nmid/upper level trough axis and accompanying area of deeper synoptic \nmoisture pivot southeastward across our region. With thermal \nprofiles remaining marginal for rain/snow (and actually warming a \nlittle compared to today due to some weak warm advection out ahead \nof the trough)...the lower elevations should see mainly wet snow to \nstart the day before changing over to mainly rain for the late \nmorning and afternoon...while the higher terrain sees more in the \nway of wet snow or a wet snow/rain mix. All of this should then \ntransition back to snow and diminish in coverage Saturday night with \nnocturnal cooling and the departure of the trough axis/deeper \nmoisture aloft. Snowfall accums Saturday and Saturday night should \nrange from an inch or two across the higher terrain to little or \nnothing across the lower elevations.\n\nSunday and Sunday night ridging at the surface and aloft will \ngradually build eastward into our region...resulting in slowly \nlowering inversion heights and a commensurate drying out of the \nprime dendritic snow growth zone aloft. This will result in any \nleftover scattered orographic and lake-induced wet snow and rain \nshowers south and southeast of the lakes gradually diminishing from \nwest to east during Sunday...with mainly dry weather then following \nfor Sunday night. This being said...the upsloping and lake \ninfluences will still be enough to maintain fairly widespread low \nclouds through Sunday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather will return by Monday, with temperatures \ntrending warmer by midweek.\n\nExpansive/strengthening ridging at the surface and aloft will \ncontinue to build east from the Great Plains and across the Great \nLakes and Ohio Valley right through the middle of next week. This \nfeature will bring a return to dry weather with perhaps a few partly \nto mostly sunny days. While temperatures will remain a little on the \ncool side out ahead of the surface high on Monday...these should \nthen rebound nicely through midweek as the axis of the ridge slides \neast of our longitude and allows a warmer southerly to southwesterly \nflow to develop across our area. At this point...it still appears \nthat this should allow daytime highs to climb back to notably above \nnormal levels (i.e, from the mid 50s to the lower 60s) by next \nThursday.",
